  "title": "Protocol Explorer · Learn how the internet works",
  "text": "PROTOCOL EXPLORER\n\nLEARN HOW THE INTERNET WORKS\n\nOSI LAYER\nAPPLICATION\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n6\n7\nSearch protocols\nCtrl K\nDESIGNED BY\nDilumSanjaya\nEN\nINTERNET PROTOCOLS\nALL\n39\nCORE\n11\nAPPLICATION\n19\nSECURITY\n9\nHTTP\nHypertext Transfer Protocol\n1\nCLIENT REQUEST\nBrowser prepares the request\n2\nDNS RESOLUTION\nFinds the server’s IP address\n3\nTCP CONNECTION\nEstablishes a reliable link\n4\nHTTP EXCHANGE\nRequest and response messages\n5\nPAGE RENDERING\nBrowser displays the content\nHTTPS\nHTTP Secure\nWebSocket\nTwo-way web messaging\nWebRTC\nBrowser video calls\nSIP\nInternet phone calls\nRTSP\nIP camera streams\nRTMP\nLive streaming upload\nQUIC\nHTTP/3 over UDP\nTCP\nTransmission Control Protocol\nUDP\nUser Datagram Protocol\nIP\nInternet Protocol\nBGP\nRouting between networks\nIPv6\nAddress setup, neighbour discovery\nDNS\nDomain Name System\nDNS over HTTPS\nPrivate DNS lookups\nDNSSEC\nSigned DNS answers\nDHCP\nDynamic Host Configuration\nICMP\nInternet Control Message\nIGMP\nMulticast and TV channels\nNTP\nNetwork Time Protocol\nEthernet\nFrames and switches\nPPPoE\nBroadband logins\nWi-Fi\nJoining a network (WPA2)\nWPA3\nWi-Fi Protected Access 3\nRADIUS\n802.1X Enterprise Wi-Fi\nCaptive Portal\nHotel and café Wi-Fi logins\nSMTP\nSimple Mail Transfer Protocol\nPOP3\nPost Office Protocol v3\nIMAP\nInternet Message Access\nFTP\nFile Transfer Protocol\nSMB\nWindows file sharing\nSSH\nSecure Shell\nTLS/SSL\nTransport Layer Security\nHTTP Proxy\nForward proxy and CONNECT\nSOCKS5\nSocket Secure proxy\nReverse Proxy\nTLS offloading\nWireGuard\nA modern VPN\nIPsec\nIKEv2 and ESP\nModbus\nIndustrial control (OT)\nInternet\nClient\n192.168.1.10\nDNS Resolver\n8.8.8.8\nWeb Server\n203.0.113.10\nRoot Server\n198.41.0.4\n.com TLD Server\n192.5.6.30\nAuthoritative Server\n198.51.100.53\nMail Server\n198.51.100.25\nProxy\n192.0.2.80\nApp Server\n10.0.0.20\nFile Server\n192.168.1.20\nISP Router\nAS 64500\nTransit Router\nAS 64510\nHosting Router\nAS 64511\nSwitch\n8 ports\nAccess Server\n198.51.100.1\nIP Camera\n192.168.1.64\nTV Box\n192.168.1.30\nTheir Laptop\n192.168.0.23\nRouter\n192.168.1.1\nTheir Router\n198.51.100.200\nNETWORK\nSEQUENCE\nPROTOCOL OVERVIEW\nHTTP\nACTIVE\n\nHypertext Transfer Protocol\n\nHTTP is the protocol of the web: a client asks for a resource, such as an HTML page, and the server answers with a status code, headers and usually a body. Every exchange is one request and one response, written as readable text in HTTP/1.1.\n\nLAYER\nApplication (Layer 7)\nDEFAULT PORT\n80 (HTTPS: 443)\nTRANSPORT\nTCP\nDATA FORMAT\nText (headers + optional body)\nCOMMON METHODS\nGET, POST, PUT, DELETE, HEAD\nDEFINED IN\nRFC 9110, RFC 9112\nPACKET FLOW\nDNS\nTCP\nRequest\nResponse\nData ACKs not shown\n1×\nPLAY\nSTEP\nRESET\nNO.\tTIME\tSOURCE\tDESTINATION\tPROTOCOL\tINFO\tSIZE\n1\t0.000s\t192.168.1.10\t8.8.8.8\tDNS\tStandard query A www.example.com\t75 B\n2\t0.012s\t8.8.8.8\t192.168.1.10\tDNS\tStandard query response A 203.0.113.10\t91 B\n3\t0.024s\t192.168.1.10\t203.0.113.10\tTCP\t52344 → 80 [SYN] Seq=0\t66 B\n4\t0.041s\t203.0.113.10\t192.168.1.10\tTCP\t80 → 52344 [SYN, ACK] Seq=0 Ack=1\t66 B\n5\t0.058s\t192.168.1.10\t203.0.113.10\tTCP\t52344 → 80 [ACK] Seq=1 Ack=1\t60 B\n6\t0.058s\t192.168.1.10\t203.0.113.10\tHTTP\tGET / HTTP/1.1\t484 B\n7\t0.076s\t203.0.113.10\t192.168.1.10\tHTTP\tHTTP/1.1 200 OK (text/html), 9 segments\t12.8 KB",
